  "why": "The bullet names Mazur's topology-of-rational-points conjectures, so (A) union-of-components and (B) finiteness are the core, with (F) the abelian part. Before the snapshot, CTSSD 1997 refutes (A) only, so it is a case with a counterexample; Waldschmidt and Gvirtz settle restricted cases in journals of record. None of these is whole, so the pre-snapshot status is still open. After the snapshot, nothing touches (A), (B) or (E) over Q. Koymans-Pagano and ABHS are about Hilbert's tenth problem over rings of integers, a different question. The OpenAI Family 004 claim is about Hilbert's tenth problem over Q, which is a neighbouring problem: by the summary it uses no Diophantine model of Z in Q, so it bears on neither (B) nor (E). I mark it variant. Its own quote supports generated, but that credit does not carry over to this problem. The namesakes are variants. Fishman et al. was the close call. It confirms Waldschmidt's density conjecture for rank-1 elliptic curves, and its text does not mention Mazur. In that case the containment is close to elementary: an infinite cyclic subgroup of the circle E(R)^0 is dense once it meets that component. So their new content is the Dirichlet-type theorem, which is a different question. I answered variant rather than case.",
